# AI Customer Support Platform — Architecture

When generating or modifying code, preserve these boundaries unless the user explicitly asks to change the architecture.

## Project

AI Customer Support Platform.

```
project-root/
├── apps/
│   ├── api/                    # TypeScript Modular Monolith
│   ├── ai/                     # Python AI Service
│   └── web/                    # React Frontend
├── packages/
│   └── contracts/              # Cross-runtime DTO / API / event contracts
├── prisma/
├── tests/
├── docs/
└── .cursor/
    └── rules/
```

- Modular monolith for the TypeScript core (`apps/api`) — do not split business modules into microservices
- Separate **Python AI service** (`apps/ai`) — independently deployable; all AI implementation lives here
- Hexagonal architecture (ports and adapters) in **both** runtimes
- Event-driven
- Production-oriented; the Python AI process is the extraction-ready AI boundary

Do not recreate the project, delete existing work, or fold AI back into TypeScript.

## Core backend (`apps/api`)

- Node.js, TypeScript, modular monolith
- Hexagonal architecture: domain / application / adapters
- PostgreSQL, Prisma, Redis
- Event-driven processing
- REST / WebSocket APIs

**Business modules:** Identity/Authentication, Organizations/Multi-tenancy, Customers, Conversations, Tickets, Agents, Knowledge Base, Notifications, Analytics, Integrations.

**AI module (`modules/ai`):** integration boundary only — `AIServicePort` plus a Python client adapter. No AI implementation.

## Frontend (`apps/web`)

- React, TypeScript, Vite
- UI library defined by frontend rules
- Talks only to the TypeScript backend APIs / WebSockets
- Never talks to Postgres, Redis, private AI providers, or the Python AI service directly
- Never implements AI logic; only required UI / API integration

## AI (`apps/ai`)

- Python, FastAPI, Pydantic
- Python async processing where appropriate
- Hexagonal architecture inside the AI service
- Owns: AI orchestration, agents, RAG, embeddings, vector search, LLM integrations, prompts, tools, guardrails, AI-specific evaluation/testing

## Hexagonal layout

TypeScript business modules:

```
<module>/
  domain/
  application/
  adapters/
    inbound/
    outbound/
```

Python AI service:

```
apps/ai/
├── app/
│   ├── domain/
│   ├── application/
│   ├── adapters/
│   ├── agents/
│   ├── orchestration/
│   ├── rag/
│   ├── llm/
│   ├── embeddings/
│   ├── vector_store/
│   ├── tools/
│   ├── prompts/
│   ├── guardrails/
│   ├── evaluation/
│   └── main.py
├── tests/
├── pyproject.toml
└── README.md
```

`domain/`, `application/`, and `adapters/` are the hexagonal layers. Capability packages (`agents/`, `orchestration/`, `rag/`, `llm/`, `embeddings/`, `vector_store/`, `tools/`, `prompts/`, `guardrails/`, `evaluation/`) are AI application/domain logic. They must not import provider SDKs.

Dependency direction: **Adapters → Application → Domain**.

Domain must NOT depend on Express/Fastify, FastAPI, Prisma/ORM, SQLAlchemy, PostgreSQL, Redis, LLM SDKs, LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex, embedding/vector SDKs, external APIs, or queue libraries.

Infrastructure implements ports defined by inner layers.

## Domain

Keep business rules in entities, value objects, and domain services:

- Conversation and ticket state transitions
- Escalation, agent assignment, SLA
- Knowledge document lifecycle (ownership, permissions, status)

Never put business rules in controllers. Never put prompts, agents, RAG, or LLM orchestration in TypeScript domain.

Python AI domain covers AI concepts (retrieval policy, guardrail outcomes, tool-call intents) — not Conversation/Ticket aggregates and not Prisma models.

## Application

Use use-cases for operations.

TypeScript: `CreateConversation`, `SendCustomerMessage`, `EscalateConversation`, `AssignAgent`, `CreateTicket`, `ResolveTicket`, `AddKnowledgeDocument`, `SendNotification`.

Python AI: `GenerateAIResponse`, RAG retrieve/index, classification, summarization, agent/orchestration use cases.

Use cases coordinate domain logic, repositories, ports, and events.

## Ports

Interfaces for infrastructure/external dependencies.

TypeScript: `ConversationRepository`, `CustomerRepository`, `TicketRepository`, `KnowledgeRepository`, `AIServicePort`, `NotificationPort`, `EventBusPort`, `QueuePort`, `AuditLogPort`.

Python AI: `LLMPort`, `EmbeddingPort`, `VectorSearchPort` (Protocol/ABC).

## Adapters

Inbound: REST controllers, WebSocket handlers, webhooks, queue consumers, event consumers.

Outbound (TypeScript): PostgreSQL/Prisma repositories, Redis, email, WhatsApp, external APIs, queues, **HTTP/queue client to the Python AI service** (`PythonAIServiceAdapter`).

Outbound (Python AI): LLM providers, embeddings, vector DB, parsers — never Prisma or TypeScript business tables.

Provider examples (choose later; do not install every provider now):

```
apps/ai/app/adapters/
├── llm/
│   ├── openai/
│   ├── anthropic/
│   └── ollama/
├── embeddings/
└── vector_store/
```

Controllers stay thin.

Never: Controller → Prisma, Controller → OpenAI, Controller → business logic.

## TypeScript ↔ Python AI boundary

**Critical:** no AI implementation in the TypeScript backend.

TypeScript must NOT directly use: OpenAI SDK, Anthropic SDK, LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex, embedding SDKs, vector database SDKs for AI, AI prompt orchestration, or AI agent implementation. All of these belong in `apps/ai`.

The TypeScript AI module is only the integration boundary:

```
apps/api/src/modules/ai/
├── application/ports/AIServicePort
└── adapters/outbound/python-ai/PythonAIServiceAdapter
```

It must not contain RAG, prompts, agents, embeddings, or provider SDKs.

Preferred flow:

```
Conversation (or other TS module)
  → AIServicePort (TypeScript)
  → PythonAIServiceAdapter (HTTP or queue)
  → Python inbound adapter (FastAPI / worker)
  → AI application / orchestration
  → LLMPort / EmbeddingPort / VectorSearchPort
  → Python outbound adapter
  → LLM / embedding / vector provider
```

Python AI must not directly modify business state (no Prisma, no conversation/ticket writes).

- Correct: Python AI → Decision/result → TypeScript application use case → Domain → Event
- Incorrect: Python AI → Database / Prisma / business tables

Tool results that change tickets or conversations are executed by TypeScript use cases, then returned to Python.

Never import Python internals from TypeScript or TypeScript internals from Python. Communicate through explicit, versionable DTO / API / event contracts (`packages/contracts`).

### Synchronous (HTTP)

```
TypeScript API → HTTP → FastAPI AI service
```

Use for: AI response generation, classification, summarization, AI decisions.

### Asynchronous (events / queue)

```
TypeScript backend → event bus / queue → Python AI worker
  → AI processing → event → TypeScript backend
```

Use for: document processing, embeddings, RAG ingestion, long-running AI tasks, batch AI processing.

## RAG

All RAG implementation belongs to Python:

Document processing → parsing → chunking → embeddings → vector storage → retrieval → context construction → prompt construction → LLM → response validation.

The TypeScript Knowledge module owns business concepts: `KnowledgeDocument`, document ownership, tenant ownership, document permissions, document lifecycle.

Python owns AI processing of those documents.

## Events

The TypeScript backend owns business events. Python consumes AI-related events. Event contracts must be explicit and versionable.

TypeScript publishes (examples): `KnowledgeDocumentUploaded`, `KnowledgeDocumentProcessingRequested`, `AIResponseRequested`, `ConversationMessageReceived`.

Python may publish (examples): `AIResponseGenerated`, `KnowledgeDocumentProcessed`, `AIProcessingFailed`.

Events are facts that already happened. Commands/use-cases are requested actions.

Other business examples: `ConversationCreated`, `MessageReceived`, `MessageSent`, `ConversationEscalated`, `AgentAssigned`, `TicketCreated`, `TicketResolved`, `NotificationRequested`.

## Async processing

Background jobs/events for: document processing, embeddings, vector indexing, notifications, analytics, external integrations, long-running/retryable AI, batch AI.

Do not block customer HTTP on those. Request-scoped AI (generation, classification, summarization, decisions) may use synchronous HTTP to Python.

## Outbox / reliability

For important event-driven workflows, prefer Outbox:

Database Transaction → Business State Change → Outbox Event → Event Publisher → Consumer.

Consumers must be idempotent.

## Multi-tenancy

If tenant/organization support exists:

- Isolate every tenant-owned resource
- Enforce isolation on the backend; never rely on frontend filtering
- Never allow cross-tenant retrieval
- Validate tenant context on every tenant-scoped operation
- Propagate tenant context from TypeScript to Python
- Every AI/RAG operation must include enough tenant context to enforce isolation
- Python must never retrieve data across tenants

## Authorization

Separate authentication from authorization. Enforce role/permission checks server-side.

Example permissions: `conversation.read`, `conversation.assign`, `conversation.escalate`, `ticket.manage`, `knowledge.manage`, `analytics.view`.

## Database

PostgreSQL is the primary transactional database unless requirements explicitly justify another.

Prisma lives behind TypeScript repository adapters. Never expose Prisma models to domain logic. Python AI never uses Prisma.

Preferred: Domain Entity → Repository Interface → Prisma Repository → PostgreSQL.

Avoid direct Prisma calls from controllers, use cases, or domain.

## Redis

Allowed for caching, rate limiting, queues, temporary state, distributed locks.

Do not use Redis as the primary transactional store without explicit justification.

## Validation

Validate all external input: HTTP, WebSocket, webhooks, queue messages, AI output, external API responses, uploaded files.

Never trust LLM output. Schema-validate AI structured output (Pydantic in Python, then again at the TypeScript boundary) before business logic uses it.

## AI tool calling

Tools must be explicitly defined and authorized. Examples: `getCustomerDetails`, `getOrderDetails`, `createTicket`, `updateTicket`, `checkRefundStatus`, `handoffToAgent`.

Flow: Python LLM → Tool Request → TypeScript validates/authorizes → TypeScript application use case → Domain → Tool Result → Python LLM.

Never give the LLM unrestricted database or infrastructure access. Never execute mutating tools inside the Python service.

## Security

Always: validate input, enforce authorization and tenant isolation, protect secrets via environment variables, rate-limit sensitive APIs, validate webhooks, avoid logging secrets or unnecessary sensitive data, protect against prompt injection, restrict AI tool permissions.

Never hardcode API keys, passwords, JWT secrets, database credentials, LLM keys, or webhook secrets. Use environment variables and `.env.example`.

## Errors

Use domain/application-specific errors (`ConversationNotFoundError`, `UnauthorizedConversationAccessError`, `InvalidConversationStateError`, `TicketNotFoundError`, `AIProviderError`, `RateLimitExceededError`).

Do not expose stack traces or infrastructure details to clients.

## Observability

Structured logging with correlation IDs. Track where useful: `requestId`, `tenantId`, `conversationId`, `messageId`, `eventId`, `jobId`, AI model, token usage, AI latency, AI cost, external API latency.

## Testing

TypeScript: domain, application, API, repositories, integration, event processing; tool authorization and invalid-AI-payload rejection at `AIServicePort`.

Python: AI domain, orchestration, agents, RAG, retrieval, prompts, tool calling, guardrails, LLM adapters, AI evaluation — pytest; fake ports; no live providers in unit tests.

Do not require real LLM calls in normal unit tests.

## Frontend

Organize by business features (conversations, tickets, customers, knowledge, agents, analytics).

Frontend talks only to backend APIs/WebSockets. Never connect frontend to databases or private AI providers.

## Real-time

WebSockets/SSE for new messages, AI responses, agent assignment, escalation, ticket updates, typing indicators.

Keep real-time transport separate from business logic.

## Naming

Prefer business-specific names: `CreateConversationUseCase`, `ConversationRepository`, `ConversationEscalatedEvent`, `AIServicePort` (TypeScript), `PythonAIServiceAdapter` (TypeScript), `LLMPort` (Python), `PostgresConversationRepository`, `OpenAILLMAdapter` (Python).

Avoid: `CommonService`, `HelperService`, `DataManager`, `GenericRepository`.

## Shared code

Keep shared TypeScript code minimal in `packages/shared`: `Result`, `DomainError`, `Logger`, `RequestContext`, `Pagination`, `EventBus`. Do not move that into Python.

`packages/contracts` holds cross-runtime DTO / API / event contracts only. Do not dump module internals or AI prompts there.

## Implementation routing

When implementing an **AI feature**:

- Python code goes under `apps/ai`
- TypeScript only gets the required integration / contract changes
- React only gets required UI / API integration
- Never implement AI logic in TypeScript

When implementing a **normal business feature**:

- TypeScript modular monolith remains the primary implementation
- Do not move business logic into Python

## Development sequence

Before implementing a feature:

1. Identify the correct module **and runtime** (TypeScript `apps/api` vs Python `apps/ai`)
2. Identify domain rules
3. Define the use case
4. Define required ports
5. Implement domain/application logic
6. Implement adapters
7. Add events if required
8. Add validation
9. Add tests
10. Add observability

Do not start by putting everything in controllers.

## Golden rule

Business logic must remain independent of frameworks, databases, AI providers, queues, and external services.

Infrastructure must be replaceable without rewriting the core business domain. AI providers must be replaceable without rewriting AI orchestration/domain.
